dcomp: gate the raw frame dumper behind NEUTRON_DCOMP_DUMP (default OFF)

The BGRA frame dumper in the cross-process capture path had NO gate. The `diag`
flag beside it is `(n % 120 == 1)` -- a rate limiter, not an env check -- and the
CreateFileW/WriteFile block sat outside even that. Its "dump each swapchain once"
guard keys seen[] on a swapchain POINTER, which the allocator reuses, so 16 slots
kept re-arming.

Measured on the dev box: 538 files / 3.9 GB of C:\nsc_*.bgra written into the
user's prefix between 2026-07-04 and 08-03, each a multi-megabyte SYNCHRONOUS
write issued from inside the 16 ms blit thread.

Same defect class as the ungated nvcuda sampler that crashed every Premiere CUDA
export the day before (INCIDENTS.md 2026-08-08): a debug feature that ships armed.

Absent -> OFF, "1" -> ON. Deliberately the opposite default to neutron_dcomp_direct()
right above it, because this one writes megabytes to disk in a present path.

--- a/dlls/dcomp/device.c
+++ b/dlls/dcomp/device.c
@@ -423,6 +423,29 @@
     return v;
 }
 
+/* ---- NEUTRON DCOMP FRAME DUMP (env NEUTRON_DCOMP_DUMP=1) --------------------
+ * Writes the raw BGRA of a captured swapchain frame to C:\nsc_<sc>_<w>x<h>.bgra.
+ * A debugging aid ONLY. Absent -> OFF; "1" -> ON. Opposite default to the gate above,
+ * because this one WRITES MULTI-MEGABYTE FILES from inside the 16 ms blit thread.
+ *
+ * Why it needs a gate at all: it never had one. The `diag` flag it sat next to is
+ * `(n % 120 == 1)`, a rate limiter rather than an env check, and the write block was
+ * outside even that. Its "once per swapchain" guard keys seen[] on a swapchain POINTER,
+ * which the allocator reuses -- so 16 slots produced 538 files / 3.9 GB in the user's
+ * prefix over a month. Found by the ungated-diagnostic audit after the nvcuda sampler
+ * crashed every Premiere CUDA export (INCIDENTS.md 2026-08-08). */
+static int neutron_dcomp_dump(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0)
+    {
+        char b[8];
+        /* absent -> OFF; "1" -> ON */
+        v = (GetEnvironmentVariableA("NEUTRON_DCOMP_DUMP", b, sizeof(b)) && b[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0;
+    }
+    return v;
+}
+
 /* Create the shared pixel buffer if it does not exist yet. Hoisted out of the readback so
  * it can run BEFORE the D3D device lock is taken -- file-mapping creation has no business
  * happening under that lock. */
@@ -787,7 +810,18 @@
                             swapchain, hwnd, desc.Width, desc.Height, px[(SIZE_T)(H / 2) * W + W / 2],
                             distinct, dd, dd >= 3 ? "HAS-VARIATION" : "TRULY-UNIFORM");
                     /* Dump EACH distinct swapchain once (keyed by swapchain ptr), + again if it gains
-                     * variation, so we SEE every ForHwnd swapchain's real content. */
+                     * variation, so we SEE every ForHwnd swapchain's real content.
+                     *
+                     * NEUTRON 2026-08-09: now gated behind NEUTRON_DCOMP_DUMP=1, default OFF.
+                     * It was not gated at all. The `diag` flag beside it is `(n % 120 == 1)` -- a
+                     * rate limiter, NOT an env gate -- and this WriteFile block sat outside even
+                     * that. The "once per swapchain" intent is defeated because seen[] is keyed on a
+                     * swapchain POINTER, which the allocator reuses. Measured on the dev box: 538
+                     * files / 3.9 GB of C:\nsc_*.bgra written into the user's prefix between
+                     * 2026-07-04 and 08-03, each a multi-megabyte SYNCHRONOUS write from inside a
+                     * 16 ms blit thread. Same class as the ungated nvcuda sampler that crashed every
+                     * Premiere CUDA export -- see INCIDENTS.md 2026-08-08. */
+                    if (neutron_dcomp_dump())
                     {
                         static struct { void *sc; int best; } seen[16]; static int nseen;
                         int si, slot = -1;
